# E2E verification (Playwright)

Same **workflow** as [cal.com’s Playwright setup](https://github.com/calcom/cal.com/blob/main/playwright.config.ts) (preflight → seed → build → tests), but the **`webServer`** entrypoint is **`node .next/standalone/server.js`** (see `playwright.config.ts`) so e2e matches the **standalone** deploy path, not `next start`. CI uses the **blob** reporter and **`npx playwright merge-reports`** after matrix shards per [Playwright sharding](https://playwright.dev/docs/test-sharding). **`IS_E2E=1`** on the Playwright web server (see `Env.ts`), parallel workers; short default timeouts — `PLAYWRIGHT_SLOW=1` when debugging locally.

## Before claiming e2e is done

1. Run **`npm run test:e2e`** from repo root for a full claim; if you cannot run it, say what was **not** verified.
2. On failure: name the test and summarize the error (last ~40 lines of output is enough); note Docker/port blockers.
3. A **partial** run (single spec or `npm run e2e` only) is **not** a substitute for the full `npm run test:e2e` when the whole e2e task is claimed complete.
4. Default port **`3008`** (`PLAYWRIGHT_E2E_PORT`). Playwright config does **not** use `.env` **`PORT`**.
5. **CI:** `E2E_SKIP_BUILD=1` when `.next` is restored from the build job; locally the first run uses `e2e:build`.
6. **Browsers:** the **PR merge gate** runs **Chromium only** (by design — speed and parity with production Chromium). `postinstall` installs Chromium locally; CI uses `npx playwright install chromium --with-deps` per [Playwright CI guidance](https://playwright.dev/docs/ci). **Firefox/WebKit are not part of the default gate**; to run the `firefox` project, set `PLAYWRIGHT_INCLUDE_FIREFOX=1`, install that browser (`npx playwright install firefox --with-deps`), then run `npx playwright test --project=firefox` after the usual preflight + build.

## This repo

- **Do not** import the app Prisma client in `tests/e2e/*` — use `pg` `Pool` (see `AccountLockout.e2e.ts`). Form errors: `formAlert` from `tests/helpers/e2e-alert.ts` (avoids clashing with the route announcer’s `role="alert"`).
